<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Patan Durbar Square</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Patan Durbar Square is the old royal square of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur">Lalitpur</a> (Patan) in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, in front of the palace of the Malla kings of Lalitpur. Most of its temples and palace courtyards date from the 17th and 18th centuries, among them the stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> Mandir, and the palace now houses the Patan Museum. It is part of the Kathmandu Valley <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/patan-durbar-square.jpg" alt="Patan Durbar Square" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zulufive · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Patan_durbar_square.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The square, paved with red bricks bordered by stone, lies at the centre of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur">Lalitpur</a>. Its main temples stand opposite the western face of the palace, with their entrances facing east towards it, and a bell stands in line beside them; old Newar houses also line the square. The city around it, a centre of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, has 136 bahals, or courtyards, and 55 major temples, most of them near the square.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The early history of the square is unclear. The site was an ancient crossroads, and the Pradhanas, who settled there before the Mallas, have connections with it. Some chronicles hint that the Thakuri dynasty built a palace there, though there is little evidence of this, and there is evidence that Patan was founded by the Kirat dynasty in the 3rd century BCE and expanded in the sixth century. Most of the present buildings date from the 1600s, under King Siddhi Narsingh Malla and his son Srinivasa Sukriti; Purandarasimha, Sivasimha Malla and Yoganarendra Malla also improved the square.</p><p>The Krishna Mandir, a three-storey temple in the Granthakuta form of the Shikhara style, was built by Siddhi Narsingh Malla in 1667; it is said that the king saw Krishna and Radha standing before his palace one night and ordered a temple on that spot. Carvings on its beams tell the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>. The Vishwanath Temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> dates from 1627, the Taleju temple of the Malla kings' personal deity from 1640, rebuilt in 1667 after a fire, and the Bhimsen Temple, with its three golden windows, from 1680.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The palace has three main courtyards: Mul Chowk, the largest, with the Vidya Temple at its centre; Keshav Narayan Chowk to its north, inside the Patan Museum; and Sundari Chowk to its south, with a sunken water tank, the Tusha Hiti. The square is one of the three Durbar Squares of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> that are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>, and it was heavily damaged by the earthquake of April 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Patan Durbar Square” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Patan became part of the Kingdom of Nepal, and its <b>Durbar Square</b> is a World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lalitpur">Kingdom of Lalitpur</a></li></ul></section>
